.TH STYXDIRTEST 1
.SH NAME
styxdirtest \- check that a 9P "do not change" field survives decoding
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B styxdirtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Styxdirtest
packs a
.B Sys->Dir
whose fields are all ones, unpacks it through
.IR styx (2)'s
.BR unpackdir ,
and requires each one to come back as
.BR ~0 .
.PP
All ones in a 9P
.B wstat
means "do not change this field".  Built into a Limbo
.BR int ,
which is 64 bits at runtime, an all-ones 32-bit field decodes to
4294967295 rather than
.BR ~0 ,
and every consumer written as
.PP
.EX
	if (d.mtime != ~0)
		onto.mtime = d.mtime;
.EE
.PP
then takes the sentinel for a real time and writes it.  0xFFFFFFFF
seconds from 1970 is 2106-02-07, which is where a file dated 2106 comes
from.  Eight files in this tree test a dir field against
.B ~0
alone;
.IR kfs (4)
tests both forms, which is someone having met this and patched the
consumer rather than the boundary.
.PP
The last check is the one that stops the fix being worse than the bug: a
real
.B mtime
has to round-trip unchanged.  The width matters in the reporting too \(em
.B %d
and
.B %x
both show the low 32 bits, so 4294967295 and
.B ~0
both print as \-1, and a failing line can read "got \-1, want \-1".  The
numbers are widened to
.B big
before printing for that reason.
.PP
It exits non-zero if any check fails, and prints a count either way.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/styxdirtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR styx (2),
.IR styxbench (1)
.SH BUGS
It checks the five narrow fields and the sentinel, not the whole of
.BR unpackdir .
